RULE NUMBER
021

TITLE
Forge: Author the Component Logo (logo.svg)

ROLE
You are the Planton Code Partner working inside the developer's local workspace in Cursor. This rule authors the newly forged component's `logo.svg` -- the glyph every console card, catalog row, wizard header, and diagram node wears for this kind. On a diagram the icon is the one identity signal nothing else supplies: color says the family, shape says the nature, only the glyph says WHICH thing. A chart built from one product's family (an instance, its database, its user) must never read as a row of identical marks.

SCOPE
- Creates `catalog/<provider>/<kindfolder>/logo.svg` (the COMPONENT ROOT, beside `README.md` and `catalog.md`)
- Does not modify any other existing files

PREREQUISITES
- The component's spec exists and its kind is registered (rules 001 and 014 complete), so the gate can see the kind
- The provider's official icon library, when the provider publishes one FOR USE IN THIRD-PARTY DIAGRAMS. Per provider:
  - Google Cloud: offers its icons for diagrams and documentation (`https://cloud.google.com/icons`; the "legacy" set is the flat blue product-icon style the catalog uses; the 2025 "core products" set only for products absent from it). Official marks are used.
  - Cloudflare: publishes product icons in its docs and dashboard packages, but its trademark guidelines reserve its logos and icons (word marks only without written permission) and the docs repository's license excludes them. **No official mark is used; every Cloudflare kind is Planton-drawn.**
  - Kubernetes: the Kubernetes Icons Set (`kubernetes/community/icons`) is published for architecture diagrams under Apache-2.0 or CC-BY-4.0 -- a copyright license that also permits derivative work -- for the objects Kubernetes itself defines (Deployment, Service, ConfigMap, ...). **Built-in objects wear the official resource icon (the `unlabeled` variant; the `labeled` one is three-letter text that dies at 18px); built-in objects the set does not cover are Planton-drawn on the set's own seven-sided base so the frame keeps meaning "a Kubernetes object". Everything hosted ON Kubernetes -- another project's software, its children, its operators -- is Planton-drawn with no frame.**
  - Auth0: its brand assets live behind Okta's partner-credentialed brand portal (`brandfolder.com/okta-brand`; the Auth0 branding-updates post directs partners to `partners@okta.com` for icons), and it publishes no icon set for the objects inside a tenant. **No official mark is used; every Auth0 kind -- a client, a connection, a resource server, a role, an action, an event stream -- is an object inside a tenant, none IS Auth0, and all are Planton-drawn.** The old badge the six kinds shared was Auth0's own trademark, the one thing the law forbids copying.
  - OpenFGA: a CNCF incubating project whose marks live in `cncf/artwork` under the CNCF trademark policy (use permitted with attribution, linking, and no-recolor conditions -- the obligations the law declines), and it publishes no icon set for its objects. **No official mark is used; a store, an authorization model, and a relationship tuple are objects inside an OpenFGA server, none IS OpenFGA, and all are Planton-drawn.** The old file the three kinds shared imitated the OpenFGA mark's dots and dumbbells on its dark tile.
  - Stripe: its logos, badges and wordmark are published at `stripe.com/newsroom/brand-assets`, where every brand element "is subject to Stripe's Marks Usage Agreement" and the wordmark may appear only in its three stated colors; it publishes no icon set for the objects inside an account. **No official mark is used; a webhook endpoint, a portal configuration, a payment-method configuration, a product or a price is an object inside a Stripe account, none IS Stripe, and all are Planton-drawn.**
  - AWS: the AWS Architecture Icons (`https://aws.amazon.com/architecture/icons/`) are offered to customers and partners "to create architecture diagrams", unaltered (trademark guideline 9: no change of proportion, color, or elements), in a quarterly asset package. The package holds two forms and the catalog uses both, each for what AWS drew it for. **A kind that IS an AWS service wears its Architecture Service icon** -- the square tile in the service category's color with a white glyph (`Architecture-Service-Icons_<release>/Arch_<Category>/64/Arch_<Service>_64.svg`). **A kind that IS an AWS resource the package draws wears its Resource icon** -- a glyph in the category color on a transparent ground, one file that reads on both themes (`Resource-Icons_<release>/Res_<Category>/Res_<Service>_<Resource>_48.svg`); the package's `Res_General-Icons` folder still ships light and dark pairs and is never used (the one-file rule below). **Everything else -- a child with no resource icon, a configuration object, a grant, a settings kind -- is Planton-drawn in the resource form**: the 48 grid, the category color, no tile, so a drawn child stands beside its official siblings as one family. The catalog's copies are re-sourced from the current package on each judgment (the pre-law files mixed two icon generations, one with gradient tiles); a `<desc>` names the package path. Two Planton kinds (the AWS runner and, on Azure, its twin) wear the brand tile.
  - Azure: Microsoft "permits the use of these icons in architectural diagrams, training materials, or documentation" (`https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/architecture/icons/`; don't crop, flip, rotate, or distort them, and don't use them to represent your own product). The set (`Azure_Public_Service_Icons_V<n>.zip`, one folder per portal category, the same icon filed under several categories) draws most portal resources, not only services -- a subnet, a route table, a disk, a key, a storage container, an alert rule each have their own -- so **a kind that IS a service or a resource Microsoft draws wears its icon** (its `<desc>` names the package path), and **everything else is Planton-drawn on the set's own 18 grid, flat**, in Azure blue (`#0078d4`) with cyan (`#50e6ff`) as the one accent and white for cut-outs: no gradients (the official icons are gradient-filled; a drawing never imitates the rendering, only the grid and the palette, so official and drawn read as kin without a drawn file passing for an official one). Microsoft ships one drawing for two portal resources in at least one place (Event Grid Domains and Event Grid Subscriptions); the gate catches it and the child is drawn. `azureplantonrunner` wears the brand tile.
  - DigitalOcean: its trademark usage guidelines (`https://www.digitalocean.com/legal/trademark-usage-guidelines`) cover "the DigitalOcean name and our product and service names, logos, taglines, and other symbols" and require its "express written permission" for any use of its logos or designs outside the guidelines. **No official mark is used; every DigitalOcean kind is Planton-drawn** on the shared 24 grid in DigitalOcean blue. The old files were DigitalOcean's own product icons, copied.
  - Any other provider: read its trademark or brand-asset terms before classifying a single kind, record the finding in this list, and draw everything until the terms say otherwise.

  Every provider in the catalog has now been read and classified; a new provider joins this list before its first kind is forged.
- **An official icon is worn only when ONE file reads on both a light and a dark plinth.** A catalog logo is one CDN-served file rendered in an image element that cannot follow the console's theme switch (the reason the brand mark is a self-contained tile). An icon a provider publishes only as a light/dark pair (AWS's General Icons, the pre-2025 AWS Resource icons) cannot be worn unaltered, and recoloring is the alteration every provider's terms forbid, so such a kind is Planton-drawn.

THE LAW
One kind, one glyph.
- A kind wears an official mark only when it IS a product of the provider it belongs to (or an object the provider itself draws an icon for), only WHERE THAT PROVIDER OFFERS ITS ICONS FOR THIS USE (the list above), and only when one file of that icon reads on both themes. Copy the vector; drop only the fixed `width`/`height` so the `viewBox` scales; never recolor, badge, crop, or combine it. A child of a product (a database inside an instance, a job of a service, a record in a zone) is NOT the product and does not wear its mark -- it wears its own official resource icon where the provider draws one, and a Planton-drawn glyph where it does not.
- Software of ANOTHER project hosted on a provider (a Postgres cluster, a Kafka broker, a service mesh, an operator that runs on Kubernetes) is not the provider's product and is Planton-drawn, whatever that project's own trademark terms would allow: a glyph that says what the software IS (a relational store, a stream platform, a woven mesh), never the project's mark and never a drawing that resembles it. This keeps every catalog logo under one owner's terms and free of the attribution, linking, and no-recolor obligations that project marks carry onto every surface that shows them.
- Where the provider does not offer its icons, every kind of that provider wears a Planton-drawn glyph, and no file under that provider names the `Official` class.
- Planton's own kinds (a Planton runner, the Planton platform, the Planton operator) wear the Planton brand mark: copy `logo.svg` byte-identical from any sibling Planton kind. Never redraw the P.
- Every other kind wears a Planton-drawn glyph that says what THAT kind is. A drawn glyph never contains, modifies, or resembles a provider's or a project's mark -- an obviously derived drawing of a trademark is the misuse its owner pursues, and it says nothing a plain drawing of the concept does not.
- One exception, tied to a license and not to a provider: where a provider publishes its own icon set under a license that permits derivative work (Kubernetes does), Planton-drawn glyphs for THAT provider's own objects may extend the set on its base form, and the `<desc>` says so.
- Two kinds of one provider never share a glyph, except Planton's own kinds sharing Planton's mark.
- Every file carries its provenance in a `<desc>` whose first words name its class (below). When a provider that offers its icons later publishes one for a kind wearing a drawn glyph, the official one replaces it (the update rule carries this).

SEQUENTIAL STEPS

1) **Decide the class**
   Does the provider offer its icons (the list above), and is this kind the provider's own product or object its library has an icon for (Cloud SQL, Cloud Run, Spanner; a Kubernetes Deployment)? Then `official`. Is it a Planton product? Then `brand`. Otherwise `drawn` -- including every child kind of a product, every configuration object, every grant, every kind of a provider that does not offer its icons, and every piece of another project's software hosted on the provider (and its operators).

2) **Official: copy the mark**
   Take the SVG from the provider's library, drop `width`/`height`, replace any editor `<title>` with
   `<desc>Official <Provider> product icon: <library>/<file></desc>` as the first child of `<svg>`
   (for a provider's own object icon set: `<desc>Official <Provider> resource icon: <library>/<file> (<license>)</desc>`; AWS's two forms read `Official AWS architecture service icon: <package path>` and `Official AWS architecture resource icon: <package path>`). Change nothing else. The `<desc>` is the ONE edit an official file receives, and the gate compares drawings with `<desc>`, `<title>`, comments, and whitespace removed -- so two kinds wearing one official icon under two descriptions are still one glyph and still fail.

3) **Brand: copy the tile**
   `cp catalog/kubernetes/kubernetesplantonrunner/logo.svg catalog/<provider>/<kindfolder>/logo.svg`. Its `<desc>` already reads `Planton brand mark`.

4) **Drawn: draw the concept**
   The style the catalog's drawn glyphs share:
   - `<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24">`; content inside the 2..22 square; no `width`/`height`. **Where the provider's own resource icons set a grid, draw on it instead so a drawn child sits beside an official sibling as one family**: AWS glyphs are `viewBox="0 0 48 48"`, content inside 4..44, 2px round-capped strokes; Azure glyphs are `viewBox="0 0 18 18"`, content inside 1.5..16.5, filled shapes with 1.1px accents.
   - the provider's icon palette only, two tones at most plus `#ffffff` for cut-outs, chosen so the glyph reads on BOTH a light and a dark plinth (Google Cloud: `#4285f4`, `#669df6`, `#aecbfa`; Cloudflare: its brand orange `#f6821f` with `#fbad41` as the second tone -- its dashboard blue `#0051c3` was tried and vanishes on a dark plinth; Kubernetes: its blue `#326ce5` with `#74a5f4` as the second tone -- a lighter `#8fb5f5` was tried and washes out on the light plinth; Auth0: its brand orange `#eb5424` with `#f27b53` as the second tone -- a paler `#f5936c` was tried and faded on the light plinth; OpenFGA: a deep green `#1fa971` with `#5fd49b` as the second tone, the hue of its mark's neon green `#8bff95`, which itself vanishes on a light plinth; AWS: ONE tone, the service category's color as the architecture icons use it -- compute and containers `#ED7100`, networking and analytics `#8C4FFF`, database and developer tools `#C925D1`, storage and cloud financial management `#7AA116`, security and business applications `#DD344C`, application integration and management `#E7157B`, machine learning `#01A88D` -- every one of which holds on both plinths at 18px and on all nine of the console's family washes; Azure: its blue `#0078d4` with cyan `#50e6ff` as the second tone, the two colors its own icons are built from, the blue body dimmest on the dark network wash and still legible there; DigitalOcean: its blue `#0069ff` with `#8dc1ff` as the second tone, both holding on both washes at 18px; Stripe: its blurple `#635bff` with `#a5a0ff` as the second tone, the blurple the wordmark itself uses on light backgrounds); no gradients, no text, no embedded raster, no provider or project mark inside
   - filled shapes or 2px round-capped strokes; one idea per glyph, from one vocabulary every provider draws from in its own palette: a relational store is a cylinder with table rows, a relational cluster two cylinders, a columnar store a cylinder sliced into standing columns, a document store a stack of pages, a graph store nodes joined by edges, a vector store vectors fanning from a query point, a cache a key, a search index a lens over what it searches, an object store a pail, a secrets vault a door with a dial, a broker one publisher fanning out to subscribers, a broker with queues an exchange feeding queue boxes, a stream platform a bundle of partitioned pipes and a topic one pipe with partitions, a schema registry a brace pair, a service mesh a woven lattice, a policy that guards a shield (with a check, a handshake, or a key for what it checks), a filter a nozzle in the traffic line, a certificate a sealed sheet with ribbons, an issuer a stamp, a secret store a cabinet of drawers, a dashboard a screen of charts, logs label ticks with uneven lines, traces spans stepping down a waterfall, a metrics stack a dial gauge, a collector a funnel into a pipe, a node pool a rack of servers, a provisioner a server rising on an arrow, an autoscaler a rack with plus and minus, a workflow a directed graph of tasks, a pipeline segments with checks, a build server a gear with a play mark, a registry containers on a shelf, a package a box with a lid, a user a person, a key a key, an alert a bell, a probe a pulse, a sink a funnel, a schedule a clock badge, a grant a small person badge on the thing granted; for an identity provider's objects: an application that signs users in an app window with a person inside, a connection users arrive through an open door with an arrow entering, a protected API a server block with a shield at its side, a role an identity badge card listing what it bundles, a login action a bolt between code brackets, an event stream events travelling down a pipe out to a destination; for an authorization engine's objects: a store a chest holding the relationships written into it, an authorization model a schema of object types joined by their relations, a relationship tuple a user related to an object; for a payment account's objects: a webhook endpoint an event arrow landing on a hook, a customer portal configuration a portal window whose rows are toggles, a payment-method configuration a stack of payment cards beside a toggle column. A cluster-wide variant of a namespaced kind stands over a bar that spans the width (a ClusterIssuer, a ClusterSecretStore). An operator (a controller that reconciles something) draws the thing it reconciles as its primary form inside a reconcile loop -- two arrows around it -- in the second tone. Kinds of one family share a base shape (every certificate is a sealed sheet, every device profile a laptop) and differ in the PRIMARY form, not only in a corner badge -- a badge alone does not separate two glyphs at 18px.
   - recognizable at 18px without its label, and distinct at 18px from every other glyph in the provider -- 18px is the size an attachment plate draws the icon at, the smallest in the console (a chip or globe draws it at 26px, a card at 34px)
   - first child: `<desc>Planton-drawn glyph: <what it depicts, one line></desc>` -- the next reader learns the intent from the file
   Judge it on the provider's contact sheet before you keep it: `go run ./tools/catalog-logo-sheet/ -provider <provider>` writes an HTML sheet of every logo at 18, 26, 34, and 48px on a light and a dark wash; open it in a browser. A glyph that does not read at 18px on both washes is redrawn, not shipped. When the kind has no diagram card yet (a set judged before its provider's diagram identities exist), the platform supplies its nine family washes through `-washes` so the glyph is judged on every plinth it could ever wear; the tool itself knows no family.

5) **Run the gate**
   `go test ./pkg/cataloglogo/` -- three rules, each failure saying what to do:
   | Failure | Meaning | Fix |
   |---|---|---|
   | `malformed-svg` | browsers will render a broken image | fix the XML (root `<svg>`, no control bytes, no `--` in comments, no duplicate attributes) |
   | `missing-provenance` | the `<desc>` does not open with a class | add the `<desc>` per step 2, 3, or 4 |
   | `shared-glyph` | another kind of this provider wears the same drawing (compared with `<desc>`, `<title>`, comments, and whitespace removed) | draw this kind's own glyph (or, if this kind IS the product and the other is its child, redraw the child); a different `<desc>` is never the fix |
   Never add a new kind to `pkg/cataloglogo/baseline.yaml`: the baseline was the debt of providers judged before the law, it only ever shrank, and it is empty now that every provider is judged -- a new kind arrives with its own glyph and its `<desc>`, or the gate refuses it.

OUTPUT
- `catalog/<provider>/<kindfolder>/logo.svg` carrying its provenance `<desc>`
- The gate green with no new baseline entry

VERIFICATION
- `go test ./pkg/cataloglogo/ ./pkg/anatomy/` green
- The file opens as an image in a browser and reads at 24px beside the provider's other logos
